# SSO and OIDC

Wyrd federates two kinds of identity from external OIDC providers: humans who log in through single sign-on, and machine workloads that present a platform-issued JWT. Both are declared the same way, as a trusted issuer, and distinguished by one field: `principal_kind`. Human issuers back the login flow. Workload issuers back the `jwt-bearer` grant.

Trusted issuers and workload bindings are configured as TOML arrays in the same `server.toml` the rest of [Configuration](/self-hosting/configuration/) uses (`$WYRD_CONFIG_PATH`, `/etc/wyrd/server.toml`, or `~/.config/wyrd/server.toml`). They have no environment-variable form. Any deployment that declares either one must also set `WYRD_SERVER_TENANT_SLUG` so the server knows which tenant they belong to (see [the implicit tenant](/self-hosting/configuration/#the-implicit-tenant)).

## Human single sign-on

A person logs in through your IdP with the OIDC authorization-code flow, secured by PKCE. Wyrd stores the login state server-side for at most five minutes and verifies the returned ID token before issuing its own tokens. The [login flow](/concepts/authentication/#human-oidc-login-flow) in Concepts has the full sequence. To set it up:

1. Register Wyrd as an OIDC client at your IdP. Set the redirect URI to `https://<your-server>/auth/callback`.
2. Add a trusted issuer with `principal_kind = "human"`, the client id, and how Wyrd authenticates back to the IdP token endpoint.
3. Map the IdP's groups to Wyrd roles so users land with the right permissions.

```toml
[[trusted_issuers]]
issuer            = "https://idp.example.com/realms/acme"
client_id         = "wyrd"
expected_audience = "wyrd"
client_auth       = "public"
principal_kind    = "human"
default_roles     = ["reader"]

[trusted_issuers.claim_mapping]
subject = "sub"
email   = "email"
groups  = "realm_access.roles"

[trusted_issuers.group_role_map]
"wyrd-admins" = ["admin"]
"wyrd-writers" = ["writer"]
```

`client_auth` selects how Wyrd authenticates to the IdP token endpoint: `"public"` for a PKCE-only client with no secret, `{ secret_basic = "…" }` or `{ secret_post = "…" }` for a shared client secret. If you use a client secret, the server needs a [sealing key](/self-hosting/authentication/#sealing-key) to encrypt it at rest.

## Workload federation

Machine workloads (Kubernetes service accounts, GitHub Actions jobs, GCP Workload Identity, SPIFFE) present a JWT their platform issued. Wyrd verifies the assertion against the issuer's JWKS, then looks up a server-owned binding that names which Service or Agent card the workload represents. The token never names its own card; the binding does.

Two declarations are needed: a trusted issuer with `principal_kind = "workload"`, and a workload binding keyed by `(issuer, subject, audience)`.

```toml
[[trusted_issuers]]
issuer            = "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
client_id         = "wyrd"
expected_audience = "wyrd"
client_auth       = "public"
principal_kind    = "workload"

[[workload_bindings]]
issuer   = "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
subject  = "repo:acme/deploy:ref:refs/heads/main"
audience = "wyrd"
kind     = "service"
name     = "deploy-svc"
space    = "prod"
version  = "1.0.0"
```

The binding's `kind` must be `service` or `agent`. Any other kind is rejected at boot because it can never resolve a principal. The `subject` is the verified external subject claim, for example a Kubernetes service account (`system:serviceaccount:default/deploy`) or a GitHub Actions repo-and-ref subject.

The workload then exchanges its platform JWT for a Wyrd access token:

```bash
curl -X POST https://<your-server>/auth/token \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "grant_type": "urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:jwt-bearer",
    "assertion": "<platform-issued-jwt>",
    "tenant": "acme"
  }'
```

## Managing issuers at runtime

Beyond boot-time TOML, trusted issuers and workload bindings have admin CRUD routes under `/v1/admin/trusted-issuers` and `/v1/admin/workload-bindings` (both require `service_accounts:write`). Use them to add or rotate federation without a server restart. See [Cloud identity](/concepts/cloud-identity/) for the full field reference and request shapes.

## Limitations

- `private_key_jwt` client authentication to the IdP token endpoint is not yet implemented. Use `public` (PKCE) or a client secret.

